# The build context is the whole repo, and most of what is in it either does not
# belong in an image or would actively break one.

# Host-built node_modules is the dangerous one: better-sqlite3 is a native addon
# compiled against the host's Node ABI and libc, so letting it shadow the copy
# built inside the image produces an "invalid ELF header" at first query.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# Build output. Rebuilt inside the image; copying a stale .next in would also
# invalidate the layer cache on every local `npm run dev`.
.next/
**/.next/
out/
dist/
**/out/
**/dist/
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# The Prisma client is generated during the build, against the image's platform.
apps/web/generated/

# Real data and real credentials. The database and spool live on a volume, and
# an .env baked into an image is a credential leak wearing a config file.
#
# Every pattern here needs its `**/` twin. .dockerignore is NOT .gitignore: it
# matches with Go's filepath.Match against the whole path from the context root,
# and `*` does not cross a `/`. So a bare `*.db` matches ./dev.db and silently
# misses apps/web/dev.db — which is the real one, holding encrypted credentials
# and the whole index. It went into an image before this comment existed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
**/*.db
**/*.db-journal
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
spool/
**/spool/
logs/
**/logs/

# History, editor and agent scaffolding — nothing the build reads.
.git/
.gitignore
.claude/
**/.claude/
.agents/
**/.agents/
.windsurf/
**/.windsurf/
**/skills-lock.json
.DS_Store

# The snap packaging: its own build, and nothing the image reads.
snap/

# Release tooling. Same reasoning, and keeping it out means editing the release
# script does not invalidate the image's build cache.
scripts/
.github/

# Docker's own files: changing a compose file should not bust the build cache.
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
.dockerignore
